---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  type="article"
  title="How I split work between AI agents"
  description="How parallel AI agents with isolated file lanes and a human merge gate handle tasks in this repository."
>
  <article class="post">
    <header>
      <p class="kind">Process · published 12 October 2026</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">How I split work between AI agents</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        This repository runs several AI agents in parallel alongside a human merge gate.
        That workflow and process is a better signal of how Ravi works than any list of software tools.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="tickets-and-sprints">
      <h2 id="tickets-and-sprints">Tickets and sprints</h2>
      <p>
        All work in this repository is structured and tracked through GitHub Issues.
        Every ticket represents exactly one outcome and includes an explicit "Done when" list that defines the exact deliverables and acceptance criteria for the task.
        Sprints are organized as one-week milestones to keep overall progress steady and measurable across every cycle.
      </p>
      <p>
        Not every open issue can be picked up by automated workers.
        Only issues opened by Ravi are eligible for agents to work on.
        Furthermore, tickets that carry specific labels or characteristics—specifically those classified as P0, P1, size L, infra, or security—must wait for an approved label from Ravi before any agent can start work on them.
        This policy guarantees that urgent items, large tasks, infrastructure modifications, and security-sensitive changes remain paused until Ravi reviews and approves them for agent execution.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="division-of-labor">
      <h2 id="division-of-labor">Division of labor</h2>
      <p>
        Responsibilities across the repository are strictly assigned between human oversight and automated assistants according to specific role boundaries:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Ravi:</strong> Decides work priorities, approves sensitive tickets, merges pull requests, applies Terraform configurations, and owns all dashboards and credentials.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Claude (Claude Code):</strong> Handles code, infrastructure, CI, design, and Terraform, and reviews pull requests submitted by other agents.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Gemini (Jules):</strong> Writes new articles using a supplied fact list.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>ChatGPT (Codex):</strong> Performs copy edits named in an issue.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        To preserve repository security, each agent connects through its own dedicated GitHub app installed on this repository only.
        No agent holds Cloudflare, HCP Terraform, or GitHub tokens.
        All secret credentials, access tokens, and administrative rights remain strictly with Ravi.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="isolated-lanes">
      <h2 id="isolated-lanes">Isolated lanes in AGENTS.md</h2>
      <p>
        To allow parallel execution without friction or file conflicts, each agent operates strictly within a defined lane.
        A lane is a set of folders that an agent is permitted to change, explicitly written down in AGENTS.md.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because these lanes do not overlap, two agents can work at the exact same time without making conflicting edits to the codebase.
        For example, Gemini can draft a new article in its assigned directory while ChatGPT edits designated text files elsewhere, without either agent modifying the other's files or causing merge conflicts in the repository.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="branches-and-ci-guards">
      <h2 id="branches-and-ci-guards">Branches and CI guard checks</h2>
      <p>
        Strict development practices govern how code and content enter the main branch.
        Nobody pushes directly to main under any circumstances.
        Every proposed change must be developed on a dedicated branch and submitted through a pull request.
        The very first line of every pull request description must be Closes #N, referencing the exact GitHub issue number it addresses.
      </p>
      <p>
        Automated CI guard checks run on every pull request to enforce these workflow rules.
        A CI guard check fails a pull request if it:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Leaves its assigned lane by modifying files outside its permitted folder list in AGENTS.md.</li>
        <li>Lacks Closes #N on the very first line of the pull request description.</li>
        <li>Leaves an open [CHECK] marker anywhere within the proposed file changes.</li>
        <li>Contains a private IP address.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="reviews-and-the-merge-gate">
      <h2 id="reviews-and-the-merge-gate">Reviews and the human merge gate</h2>
      <p>
        Passing automated CI guard checks is only the first step in the pipeline.
        Before any change is merged, Claude reviews each pull request against the original issue's "Done when" list to verify that all stated criteria have been met.
      </p>
      <p>
        Final authority over the repository remains with Ravi.
        Ravi merges a pull request only when all automated checks are green and Claude's review has no remaining open items.
        Nothing merges itself.
        Additionally, applying Terraform infrastructure changes to live state and managing environment credentials remain manual actions performed exclusively by Ravi.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

